186503644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503644 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbd9e9ed-c643-53cb-b091-8e25e3819c29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16980bbd-b929-5d3b-bedf-b283c32f0104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503659 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f37a13c-db81-5c51-b1fe-cf49ee77cc51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503671 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-x})}^{′}+(u{e}^{-x}) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x}-u{e}^{-x}+u{e}^{-x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x}-\\cancel{u{e}^{-x}}+\\cancel{u{e}^{-x}} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74b05c82-78aa-5425-a974-d8adb06f503c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503700 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503705 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503705 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503705 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503705 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=5x-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dd414c2-6c98-512b-8e09-cef5e3ee65c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503705 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6116c6c-0d2d-5d26-9e6b-b6dd0de87989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503723 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=3x+1.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca23d1f9-e0ba-5c4f-a4c8-1fb3f4fded3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503742 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}(3x+1) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
